  .sha256: the fingerprint published by the website (to check the image);
  the stick: an empty file playing the USB stick.

ob.secrets.texte:
Online assistants often ask for an API KEY: a password for a machine.
Whoever has the key can use the service in your place (and sometimes make you pay).
The rules:
  • never in a shared file, never in code that is sent;
  • in a separate file (.env) locked with a padlock (chmod 600);
  • that file in .gitignore, so that git never takes it along;
  • or in a terminal variable (export).
Here, the key is an EXAMPLE that works nowhere. Never give a real key to a game.

ob.agent.texte:
An agent is a robot that ACTS: it reads, writes, and can run commands.
The chief keeps control:
  • they read the agent's LOG;
  • they REVIEW what the agent changed (git diff, diff);
  • they REFUSE what is dangerous;
  • they can STOP it (Ctrl+C, the emergency stop).
Robot-Tidier is waiting for you in chantier/. It is a fictional agent: it only acts there.

ob.equipe.texte:
The big task is in equipe/grande_tache.txt. Your team (fictional agents of the game):
  • Sorter tidies the guest list and removes duplicates;
  • Counter counts the guests;
  • Quill writes one invitation per guest;
  • Copier makes copies… if it doesn't run wild.
The chief splits the task, gives a clear instruction, watches, refuses, tests and can stop everything: agent stop.

dn.minimum.ou:
